| Concept        | Basic meaning                                           |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Process        | Running program with its own memory space               |
| Thread         | Execution unit inside a process                         |
| Concurrency    | Multiple tasks make progress by taking turns            |
| Parallelism    | Multiple tasks execute simultaneously on multiple cores |
| Context switch | CPU changes from one execution context to another       |
| Race condition | Result depends on timing of concurrent access           |
| Lock           | Allows controlled access to shared data                 |
| Deadlock       | Threads wait forever for each other's resources         |
| Semaphore      | Limits how many tasks can access a resource at once     |




Part 1 — Linux Practical

Now let's see the process/thread concepts in Linux.

Run:

ps -ef | head

This shows running processes.

Now:

echo $$

This shows the PID of your current shell.

Remember:

PID = Process ID

Find your Python process

Run your thread program:

python3 thread_demo.py

While it is running, open another terminal:

ps -ef | grep python

You should see your Python process.

Now inspect it with:

ps -o pid,ppid,stat,cmd -p <PID>

You can replace <PID> with the actual number.

You'll see things such as:

PID
PPID
STAT
CMD
Remember

PID → process ID
PPID → parent process ID
STAT → process state/status

Part 2 — Observe CPU/Memory

Run:

top

You'll see processes using:

CPU
MEMORY
PID

This is something you'll use constantly in DevOps/SRE.

Press:

q

to exit.

Part 3 — Kill a Process

Start something that keeps running:

sleep 300

In another terminal:

ps -ef | grep "sleep 300"

Find its PID.

Then:

kill <PID>

Check again:

ps -ef | grep "sleep 300"

The process should be gone.

You just performed a basic process lifecycle operation.

Part 8 — See Threads From Linux

Run the semaphore program:

python3 semaphore_demo.py

While it is running, find its PID and execute:

ps -T -p <PID>

This lets you see the threads belonging to that process.

Conceptually:

Process PID 5000
   ├── Thread
   ├── Thread
   ├── Thread
   ├── Thread
   └── Thread

This connects everything:

Linux process
      ↓
Python process
      ↓
Python threads
      ↓
Shared memory
      ↓
Lock / Semaphore
      ↓
Concurrency
